#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114987 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d81e3f1-d930-59ee-b439-2a3645aebb6c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Consistent System of Equations Using Gauss-Jordan Elimination"}] :question/id #uuid "dc6dd9b4-de7f-52d0-8ff9-a7a7f7a9abd7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4711" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/a50d551e5d8abe8d9ae48778b195c382bbbf96f6fc5083c41b67642e99deffda.png)\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/48c423256fbdc0d7e4f6ff716fb449efe6f98bdf57ba90353bcced930ea4ed17.png)\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/81b7626ac30b06c5eeb10be274dd93476a86dce5fe3053722d96e16b78d7f165.png)\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $z,$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $z$ in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1-5y \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1-5y \\\\ y \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114988 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "22733f71-de4a-5058-a41c-039e66e333c5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of a Constant for Which a Given Linear System Is Consistent or Inconsistent"}] :question/id #uuid "f2c7cfd3-da43-5208-bd0e-21692357fabe" :question/math-academy-id "e-4712" :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-2y=1, \\\\ 4x+ky=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/a115d3da691619918bf92d914acdfdd95088e9a141a82ca3fe3230fa7fbb8172.png)\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & k & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & k+4 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/cfea36ae2b5cb5d8715480c1e0ee0b5ba06cf39d0c2032eb6e9b9078bf8c5bde.png)\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=-4,$ then the second equation is $0=-5,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115041 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9469d5c-e097-590c-8ddb-2c7fda353fac" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent"}] :question/id #uuid "cd56314d-f45a-55be-9762-983fe507a1a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-6173" :question/problem "Given the system\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/5f1aaf29a3dd8b7ea2c4f7104d532f7b56f32a23ccebbcfa0d1bfe86c772cc5f.png)\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system is consistent dependent\n\n- The system has no solution\n\n- The system is consistent independent" :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/6171d242b0a3887802f629de79cb952d47711a581e88db2d9304a5db356dfb62.png)\n\nUsing Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 6 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14042553/assets/80aff0fd62e3e1aeed17017c864d8d0b875b556ce1a29a89e6c61e632bf9c733.png)\n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just a single equation:\n\n$$\n2x -3y= 4\n$$\n\nThe basic variable is $x,$ and we have a free variable $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*.\n\nAs a result, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d81e3f1-d930-59ee-b439-2a3645aebb6c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Consistent System of Equations Using Gauss-Jordan Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56836203-c4be-53e9-b541-53a7bedc87b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317553} {:answer/id #uuid "abf4b289-daed-5182-a993-644d739be96c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4y-3 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317554} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f6f0f7-d6ab-508e-9ece-17d869f2c1cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4y+3 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317555} {:answer/id #uuid "922b9afb-4edf-5afd-926d-9a8590705ddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4y-3 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317556} {:answer/id #uuid "ee8a01d4-8cb1-5cd3-a55e-21515404bc40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3-4y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abf4b289-daed-5182-a993-644d739be96c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4y-3 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317554} :answer-field/id #uuid "c94cbe09-bdb3-5177-b965-eab79df51cb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b0feb9ee-8e97-5292-aff6-05655689f05b" :question/math-academy-id "q-131712" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 2x+8y=-6 \\\\  & -4x-16y=12 \\\\  & 6x+24y=-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 & -6 \\\\ -4 & -16 & 12 \\\\ 6 & 24 & -18\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 8 & -6 \\\\ -4 & -16 & 12 \\\\ 6 & 24 & -18\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -3 \\\\ -4 & -16 & 12 \\\\ 6 & 24 & -18\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 24 & -18\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now  \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+4y=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$  in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-4y-3 \\\\ y=y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4y-3 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d81e3f1-d930-59ee-b439-2a3645aebb6c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Consistent System of Equations Using Gauss-Jordan Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f763111a-5af4-5488-a929-0020e0aeba2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4-5{x}_{3} \\\\ 1-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317560} {:answer/id #uuid "59bbb71e-60d8-5eae-8d64-214b3d7c235e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{3}-4 \\\\ 2{x}_{3}-1 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317561} {:answer/id #uuid "18dc88f8-bcb4-52ae-adfb-a82167f7d74d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317562} {:answer/id #uuid "d6d2bc07-7dc8-55a2-81a6-b1489f131d1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{3}+4 \\\\ 2{x}_{3}+1 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317563} {:answer/id #uuid "0c30fead-4a73-5022-9e9a-21dd0b0638a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{3}-4 \\\\ 1-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c30fead-4a73-5022-9e9a-21dd0b0638a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{3}-4 \\\\ 1-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317564} :answer-field/id #uuid "0efdee88-6a7c-5974-993d-2ab3cf0ff497" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ea253a83-404b-5d27-8e99-ab1346327cc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-131717" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-1 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -5 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -5 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -5 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe corresponding system is now  \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}-5{x}_{3}=-4 \\\\  & {x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ and we have one free variable, ${x}_{3}.$ That means we need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ in terms of ${x}_{3}.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=5{x}_{3}-4 \\\\ {x}_{2}=1-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}={x}_{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{3}$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{3}-4 \\\\ 1-2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317565 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9469d5c-e097-590c-8ddb-2c7fda353fac" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f81946b8-0e51-5d2c-a843-bc2231e1d145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317567} {:answer/id #uuid "4e841753-e6ff-5ea7-b26f-6d9575329fa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317568} {:answer/id #uuid "33e69ce1-6954-5201-8c9c-5e864e29e461" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317569} {:answer/id #uuid "2e3ae4ee-b56e-5f4e-aae8-59dacc65408d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317570} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ba308b-dbba-56f5-baa5-466e5aeb13fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317571}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5ba308b-dbba-56f5-baa5-466e5aeb13fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317571} :answer-field/id #uuid "33fce12e-12f7-5945-a801-72dea6bcf2e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317566}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "85139672-fc12-5f19-974e-cd9c9c3f59d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-131719" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+4y=-1 \\\\  & 2x+8y=-2 \\\\  & -3x-12y=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system is inconsistent\n- The system is consistent dependent\n- The system has a unique solution" :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 8 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -12 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 8 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -12 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -12 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x,$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we can find $x$ in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*. \n\nAs a result, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9469d5c-e097-590c-8ddb-2c7fda353fac" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "908a019e-8258-54e1-a3e6-de86654a1050" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317574} {:answer/id #uuid "ea552307-27bc-5f39-aeb6-7cbb6673ebb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317575} {:answer/id #uuid "1e6537cf-4f5d-52e6-94e3-f493b4f11925" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317576} {:answer/id #uuid "d27cbb7b-ab8d-5431-b448-c300eaa43995" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317577} {:answer/id #uuid "4b8dd03f-0f8a-5866-a7eb-663a28f92dd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b8dd03f-0f8a-5866-a7eb-663a28f92dd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317578} :answer-field/id #uuid "b05a5f36-6352-563f-aae4-29464953bae5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4890e1ff-ed9a-5558-acd1-bcef01439e04" :question/math-academy-id "q-131722" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 3x-3y=6 \\\\  & -2x+y=-3 \\\\  & 4x-4y=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system is consistent dependent\n- The system is consistent independent\n- The system has no solution" :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ are solutions to the third equation $0x+0y=0$, we can remove this equation from the system. So, the corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y=2 \\\\ y=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation we have $y=-1$ and substituting this into the first equation yields $x=1.$ So,  we conclude that the system is consistent independent. \n\nAs a result, only statement II is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9469d5c-e097-590c-8ddb-2c7fda353fac" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c0b9397-ce4f-5728-b49f-219395f94aac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317581} {:answer/id #uuid "b51758dc-7f53-57bf-92cf-0283d4a01944" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317582} {:answer/id #uuid "e6b7640a-48ae-5763-9b0d-2ea31e3bb516" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317583} {:answer/id #uuid "78a929b9-0473-539f-beb7-2bbefc400063" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317584} {:answer/id #uuid "9bd15515-5743-5020-981f-7c4b2f450897" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b51758dc-7f53-57bf-92cf-0283d4a01944" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317582} :answer-field/id #uuid "43dbb445-b54a-5c1b-aa3d-7705c663ae63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e93f981b-b1bd-511a-87f1-b614c74c85ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-131724" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x-2y=2 \\\\  & -3x-4y=3 \\\\  & 2x-4y=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system is consistent dependent\n- The system has a unique solution\n- The system is inconsistent" :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final row corresponds to the equation $0x+0y=-5,$ and this has no solution. Therefore, our system has no solution, and we conclude that the system is inconsistent.\n\nAs a result, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317586 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "22733f71-de4a-5058-a41c-039e66e333c5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of a Constant for Which a Given Linear System Is Consistent or Inconsistent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2192a06-07ec-530e-b67e-3bfb1e2b67a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317588} {:answer/id #uuid "7ca1c78d-d02c-5563-8c6b-d09d96b89816" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317589} {:answer/id #uuid "a56385c6-9689-5117-925e-6bdf347c8be6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317590} {:answer/id #uuid "bffb2cb5-dc8d-595a-b9ae-53d24eab107c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317591} {:answer/id #uuid "12503895-3863-5b7a-90f9-b6b70439dc0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317592}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12503895-3863-5b7a-90f9-b6b70439dc0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317592} :answer-field/id #uuid "47e2311e-69cb-5a3f-99ce-e1179d238593" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d47e9f79-9224-5415-86c6-0753b047d174" :question/math-academy-id "q-131727" :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x-3y=-1 \\\\  & 4x+3ky=3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3k & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3k & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3k+12 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-3y=-1 \\\\ (3k+12)y=7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=-4,$ then the second equation is $0=7,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317593 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "22733f71-de4a-5058-a41c-039e66e333c5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of a Constant for Which a Given Linear System Is Consistent or Inconsistent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bfe9ef6-dcef-5d1c-89ab-085d758feff3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317595} {:answer/id #uuid "4a162357-d3eb-5b79-a136-8f7482a4d4a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317596} {:answer/id #uuid "8d98839c-b0ac-51c1-aa1e-3f5c9668efe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317597} {:answer/id #uuid "8bcc81a2-c947-5975-aeea-d4f7abb1d8de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317598} {:answer/id #uuid "f90fd90e-4c48-5dc4-88a5-2254307f1681" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bcc81a2-c947-5975-aeea-d4f7abb1d8de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317598} :answer-field/id #uuid "01c086ee-bd59-5116-96cd-3ea346830057" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "676ffc31-b8a6-5563-a4f4-246e28e53f8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-131729" :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is consistent dependent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & -2x+7y=6 \\\\  & x+ky=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 7 & 6 \\\\ 1 & k & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 7 & 6 \\\\ 1 & k & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & k & -3 \\\\ -2 & 7 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & k & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2k+7 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+ky=-3 \\\\  & (2k+7)y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=-\\frac{7}{2},$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y$, and we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $x+ky=-3.$ \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=-\\frac{7}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "22733f71-de4a-5058-a41c-039e66e333c5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of a Constant for Which a Given Linear System Is Consistent or Inconsistent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af1a1043-2e39-53c4-a742-417c2d88b81b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317602} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ad8605-9941-5e6e-84db-dda2a0900836" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317603} {:answer/id #uuid "7ddc445b-eaf4-5239-b325-e67a7076a111" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317604} {:answer/id #uuid "0c29526d-5f05-5fb0-8fd1-80e4970a0ad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317605} {:answer/id #uuid "4fef6c43-56bf-5cae-8242-936f5a1353d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af1a1043-2e39-53c4-a742-417c2d88b81b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317602} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cbe41d7-612c-5263-adf8-d7662a099b57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1faa14aa-cb9d-5c62-96d4-f53593684603" :question/math-academy-id "q-131731" :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is consistent dependent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & -2x+6y=-4 \\\\  & 5x+(k-6)y=10\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & k-6 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & k-6 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{5}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 & -4 \\\\ 0 & k+9 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & -2x+6y=-4 \\\\  & (k+9)y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=-9,$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y.$ So, we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $-2x+6y=-4.$  \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=-9.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317607 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d81e3f1-d930-59ee-b439-2a3645aebb6c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Consistent System of Equations Using Gauss-Jordan Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e7a5b67-f13d-5ecd-9c55-04bf75ad9f6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5-y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317609} {:answer/id #uuid "c55855e4-fc66-5d64-a2b1-efb507fec362" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317610} {:answer/id #uuid "e07c84d7-1589-5805-8860-fbb861e4c39d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}-y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317611} {:answer/id #uuid "99cbd0d8-7b09-5efa-8f17-201122c25a1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}+x \\\\ x\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317612} {:answer/id #uuid "fd41b690-20e8-52dc-8c3c-9bb0f646fd6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{5}-2 \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e07c84d7-1589-5805-8860-fbb861e4c39d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}-y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317611} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1b6f7cb-18ee-5250-a3f9-3c9d6c108b8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "02a48d7a-4163-5713-bdfc-4c30a3ac1491" :question/math-academy-id "q-32244" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 2x+2y=5 \\\\  & -10x-10y=-25 \\\\  & 8x+8y=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -10 & -10 & -25 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 20\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -10 & -10 & -25 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 20\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -10 & -10 & -25 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 20\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+10{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 20\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-8){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now  \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+y=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$  in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{5}{2}-y \\\\ y=y,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}-y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115672 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "22733f71-de4a-5058-a41c-039e66e333c5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of a Constant for Which a Given Linear System Is Consistent or Inconsistent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99166388-f058-5f72-b80c-5b4282a522cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113433} {:answer/id #uuid "b6e9df98-67f7-54c0-8ec3-bce6cc775993" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k = \\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113434} {:answer/id #uuid "f60f9877-fd36-59d9-a3ec-527b98368e01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k = \\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113435} {:answer/id #uuid "284ec6b1-1f86-5100-be3a-3e470bb91463" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k =-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113436} {:answer/id #uuid "404830dc-4c84-5f9f-b429-3660dc356338" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k =-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f60f9877-fd36-59d9-a3ec-527b98368e01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k = \\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113435} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f40e7bb-c091-5bfa-b08f-acb863997d65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2a9a3feb-6c16-50e8-885d-ee5cd242ab95" :question/math-academy-id "q-32246" :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is consistent dependent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 3x+4y=12 \\\\  & x+ky=4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 12 \\\\ 1 & k & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 12 \\\\ 1 & k & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & k & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & k & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4-3k & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+ky=4 \\\\  & (4-3k)y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is consistent dependent if it has infinitely many solutions. For the system to have infinitely many solutions, it must have at least one free variable.\n\nIf $k=\\frac{4}{3},$ then the second equation is $0=0,$ which is true for any value of $y$, and we can remove this equation from the system. As a result, the system will contain only one equation, $x+ky=4.$ \n\nThen, the basic variable will be $x,$ and we will have a free variable $y.$ That means the system will have infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, our system is consistent dependent when $k=\\frac{4}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317614 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d81e3f1-d930-59ee-b439-2a3645aebb6c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Consistent System of Equations Using Gauss-Jordan Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8fb9412-eb4b-5fc7-a668-6254be1176b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2-z \\\\ 0 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317616} {:answer/id #uuid "180aa355-38f3-5bc4-a98d-6089535eaac0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1-z \\\\ 0 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317617} {:answer/id #uuid "43677cd0-73bf-5272-95a1-c7d210e1257c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-z \\\\ z-1 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317618} {:answer/id #uuid "3a715954-196e-5121-9eb2-a071e7214ff8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ z-1 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317619} {:answer/id #uuid "118b2c2b-be06-5fce-b85e-7beb4cab4232" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a715954-196e-5121-9eb2-a071e7214ff8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ z-1 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317619} :answer-field/id #uuid "47bbcdd9-b23e-52e8-94f0-96d651969d9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "013e11bb-39c0-595b-a244-03847638286f" :question/math-academy-id "q-42019" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x-2y+2z=2 \\\\  & y-z=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x=0 \\\\  & y-z=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=z-1 \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ z-1 \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317621 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9469d5c-e097-590c-8ddb-2c7fda353fac" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9eaa3d19-5e97-5969-be15-6d2f7e90d195" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317623} {:answer/id #uuid "233786e0-6b57-514b-998e-fa7d6da6c104" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317624} {:answer/id #uuid "14e5e29b-adbe-5916-bfa3-e152421f2417" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317625} {:answer/id #uuid "f6ff5d69-7707-5b49-863c-a61832e1f51b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317626} {:answer/id #uuid "b8035881-063f-5748-82a7-e8e17c4d482c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14e5e29b-adbe-5916-bfa3-e152421f2417" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317625} :answer-field/id #uuid "daf102e0-7cc7-5eb1-8770-e5338389ece2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a930b2a-2578-5145-8e40-06b6cab16ba9" :question/math-academy-id "q-42020" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+2y=-1 \\\\  & 3x+y=3 \\\\  & -2x+y=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system is consistent dependent\n- The system has a unique solution\n- The system is inconsistent" :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final row corresponds to the equation $0x+0y=2,$ and this has no solution. Therefore, our system has no solution, and we conclude that the system is inconsistent.\n\nAs a result, only statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317628 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d81e3f1-d930-59ee-b439-2a3645aebb6c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Consistent System of Equations Using Gauss-Jordan Elimination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a74658ab-6621-565d-8a89-7358301574eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2+4z \\\\ 1-3z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317630} {:answer/id #uuid "192b6da6-515a-5d39-94fe-459d8d85fff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1+2z \\\\ -3z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317631} {:answer/id #uuid "d956ce98-8354-52ea-b7e2-6f47d9302574" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2z \\\\ 4z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317632} {:answer/id #uuid "289b5e1b-d5de-5cd8-a853-a1325eb3ba0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2+4z \\\\ -3z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317633} {:answer/id #uuid "41109a43-3c87-59ca-bcc7-229b8a8eb449" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1+4z \\\\ 3z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "192b6da6-515a-5d39-94fe-459d8d85fff9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1+2z \\\\ -3z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317631} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc7d8608-6cac-5579-9b7d-8a78b7ebbee6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09fe1174-4802-515f-8d41-d02fb865fb55" :question/math-academy-id "q-42041" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-4z=2 \\\\ 4x+y-5z=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -5 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -5 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -5 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2z=1 \\\\ y+3z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1+2z \\\\ y=-3z \\\\ z=z,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $z$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1+2z \\\\ -3z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115743 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9469d5c-e097-590c-8ddb-2c7fda353fac" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a79c34b-32dc-5dd9-a547-05781ee4559b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113830} {:answer/id #uuid "bad97787-15c0-5eb2-8a26-558c015920c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113831} {:answer/id #uuid "be7a233f-6036-5757-ac42-a9794c8286d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113832} {:answer/id #uuid "52522f4f-4d21-50e5-b9e7-86a675684a34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113833} {:answer/id #uuid "6d5083cf-905b-57fe-8ee8-436b8ce70354" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a79c34b-32dc-5dd9-a547-05781ee4559b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113830} :answer-field/id #uuid "f23f96f3-e33d-5238-92ef-f68710e3e8bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e80c83a9-9107-528b-af57-73287d15e8e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-42061" :question/problem "Consider the following system of equations.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x-3y+z=0 \\\\  & -4x+6y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system is consistent dependent\n- The system has no solution\n- The system is consistent independent" :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 6 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 6 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we can to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*. \n\nAs a result, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317635 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "22733f71-de4a-5058-a41c-039e66e333c5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of a Constant for Which a Given Linear System Is Consistent or Inconsistent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54cdb6ed-f055-5f90-8991-c404304e62d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317637} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e1171a-84cd-57a8-a072-8fa3c77d6a38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317638} {:answer/id #uuid "253e28e1-1ed1-5ae3-9a70-7506ff88073a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317639} {:answer/id #uuid "fb329066-6cb8-5ae7-98d4-7abece7913f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317640} {:answer/id #uuid "49af443b-86d7-549e-a16b-7b7eb9af27e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb329066-6cb8-5ae7-98d4-7abece7913f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317640} :answer-field/id #uuid "aaa208d6-4930-5edd-8817-25def91020ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317636}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d0469220-01c3-5e98-b2ee-ca1c1a254981" :question/math-academy-id "q-74646" :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+4y=2 \\\\  & 2x+(k+1)y=3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k+1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & k+1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & k-7 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+4y=2 \\\\ (k-7)y=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=7,$ then the second equation is $0=-1,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=7.$"}]}
